<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-geometric-formulas">
<title>Geometric Formulas</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Basic algebra, coordinate geometry, Euclidean geometry,
trigonometry, and mathematical notation.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this reference section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>recognize standard notation for geometric quantities;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute distances and midpoints in coordinate geometry;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute slopes and equations of lines;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use formulas for angles and polygons;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute perimeter and area of common plane figures;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute circumference and area of circles;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use arc-length and sector-area formulas;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize formulas for regular polygons;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute surface areas and volumes of common solids;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize formulas for prisms, cylinders, pyramids, cones,
          spheres, and frustums;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use the Pythagorean theorem;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize special right-triangle ratios;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use distance formulas in two and three dimensions;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>compute areas using coordinates;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize vector formulas for geometric area and volume;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use equations of circles, spheres, planes, and common conics;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize parametric and polar geometric formulas;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish degrees and radians;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>use geometric similarity and scaling laws;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize formulas for centroids and centers;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand how dimensions scale area and volume;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>avoid common geometric-formula errors.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</p>
</remark>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-common-geometric-notation">
<title>Common Geometric Notation</title>
<p>Frequently used geometric symbols include</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\text{area},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P
=
\text{perimeter},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
C
=
\text{circumference},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\text{volume},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
\text{surface area},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
r
=
\text{radius},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\text{diameter or distance},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
h
=
\text{height},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\theta
=
\text{angle}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Notation varies by context, so quantities should be defined when ambiguity is
possible.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-angle-units-reference">
<title>Angle Units</title>
<p>Angles are commonly measured in degrees or radians.</p>

<p>A full revolution is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
360^\circ
=
2\pi
\text{ radians}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
180^\circ
=
\pi
\text{ radians}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-degree-radian-conversion">
<title>Degree-to-Radian Conversion</title>
<p>To convert degrees to radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\theta_{\mathrm{rad}}
=
\theta_{\mathrm{deg}}
\frac{\pi}{180}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>60^\circ
=
60
\frac{\pi}{180}
=
\boxed{
\frac{\pi}{3}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-radian-degree-conversion">
<title>Radian-to-Degree Conversion</title>
<p>To convert radians to degrees,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\theta_{\mathrm{deg}}
=
\theta_{\mathrm{rad}}
\frac{180}{\pi}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\frac{3\pi}{4}
=
\frac{3\pi}{4}
\frac{180}{\pi}
=
\boxed{
135^\circ.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-complementary-supplementary">
<title>Complementary and Supplementary Angles</title>
<p>Complementary angles satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\alpha+\beta
=
90^\circ
=
\frac{\pi}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Supplementary angles satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\alpha+\beta
=
180^\circ
=
\pi.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-angles-around-point">
<title>Angles Around a Point</title>
<p>Angles around a point sum to</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
360^\circ
=
2\pi.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus if angles <m>\theta_1,\ldots,\theta_n</m> meet around a point,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\sum_{k=1}^{n}\theta_k
=
360^\circ.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-angle-sum">
<title>Triangle Angle Sum</title>
<p>For a Euclidean triangle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A+B+C
=
180^\circ
=
\pi.
}</md>
</p>

<p>This formula depends on Euclidean geometry.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-exterior-angle">
<title>Exterior Angle of a Triangle</title>
<p>An exterior angle of a triangle equals the sum of the two remote interior
angles.</p>

<p>If exterior angle is <m>E</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
E=A+B.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-polygon-interior-angle-sum">
<title>Polygon Interior Angle Sum</title>
<p>For a simple <m>n</m>-gon,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{sum of interior angles}
=
(n-2)180^\circ.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(n-2)\pi.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-polygon-interior-angle">
<title>Regular Polygon Interior Angle</title>
<p>For a regular <m>n</m>-gon, each interior angle is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{(n-2)180^\circ}{n}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{(n-2)\pi}{n}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-polygon-exterior-angle">
<title>Regular Polygon Exterior Angle</title>
<p>Each exterior angle of a regular <m>n</m>-gon is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{360^\circ}{n}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{2\pi}{n}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-pythagorean-theorem-reference">
<title>Pythagorean Theorem</title>
<p>For a right triangle with legs <m>a,b</m> and hypotenuse <m>c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a^2+b^2=c^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c
=
\sqrt{a^2+b^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-worked-pythagorean-reference">
<title>Worked Example: Pythagorean Theorem</title>
<example>
<title>Find the Hypotenuse</title>
<p>Suppose</p>

<p>
<md>a=5,
\qquad
b=12.</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>c^2
=
5^2+12^2
=
25+144
=
169.</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
c=13.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-45-45-90-reference">
<title>Special Right Triangle: <m>45^\circ-45^\circ-90^\circ</m></title>
<p>The side ratio is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1:1:\sqrt2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If each leg has length <m>a</m>, then the hypotenuse is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a\sqrt2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-30-60-90-reference">
<title>Special Right Triangle: <m>30^\circ-60^\circ-90^\circ</m></title>
<p>The side ratio is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
1:\sqrt3:2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If the shortest side is <m>a</m>, then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{longer leg}
=
a\sqrt3
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{hypotenuse}
=
2a.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-perimeter-reference">
<title>Triangle Perimeter</title>
<p>For side lengths <m>a,b,c</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P=a+b+c.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-area-base-height">
<title>Triangle Area: Base and Height</title>
<p>For base <m>b</m> and perpendicular height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12bh.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-herons-formula-reference">
<title>Heron's Formula</title>
<p>For triangle sides <m>a,b,c</m>, define semiperimeter</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s
=
\frac{a+b+c}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Then</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\sqrt{
s(s-a)(s-b)(s-c)
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-area-trig">
<title>Triangle Area From Two Sides and an Included Angle</title>
<p>If sides <m>a,b</m> include angle <m>C</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12ab\sin C.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12bc\sin A
=
\frac12ca\sin B.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-equilateral-triangle-formulas">
<title>Equilateral Triangle</title>
<p>For side length <m>a</m>, height is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
h
=
\frac{\sqrt3}{2}a.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac{\sqrt3}{4}a^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Perimeter is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P=3a.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-rectangle-formulas">
<title>Rectangle</title>
<p>For length <m>l</m> and width <m>w</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=lw.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Perimeter is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P=2l+2w
=
2(l+w).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Diagonal length is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{l^2+w^2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-square-formulas">
<title>Square</title>
<p>For side <m>s</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=s^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P=4s,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and diagonal</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d=s\sqrt2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parallelogram-formulas">
<title>Parallelogram</title>
<p>For base <m>b</m> and perpendicular height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=bh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For adjacent sides <m>a,b</m> with included angle <m>\theta</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
ab\sin\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-rhombus-formulas">
<title>Rhombus</title>
<p>For diagonals <m>d_1,d_2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12d_1d_2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For side <m>a</m> and included angle <m>\theta</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
a^2\sin\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-trapezoid-formulas">
<title>Trapezoid</title>
<p>For parallel bases <m>b_1,b_2</m> and height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
(b_1+b_2)h.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The median or midsegment length is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m
=
\frac{b_1+b_2}{2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=mh.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-kite-formulas">
<title>Kite</title>
<p>For perpendicular diagonals <m>d_1,d_2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12d_1d_2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-polygon-perimeter">
<title>Regular Polygon Perimeter</title>
<p>For a regular <m>n</m>-gon with side length <m>s</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P=ns.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-polygon-area-apothem">
<title>Regular Polygon Area Using Apothem</title>
<p>If <m>a</m> is the apothem and <m>P</m> is perimeter,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12aP.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus for side length <m>s</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12a(ns).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-polygon-area-side">
<title>Regular Polygon Area From Side Length</title>
<p>For a regular <m>n</m>-gon with side length <m>s</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac{
ns^2
}{
4\tan(\pi/n)
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circle-radius-diameter">
<title>Circle Diameter and Radius</title>
<p>For a circle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d=2r.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
r=\frac{d}{2}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circle-circumference-reference">
<title>Circle Circumference</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
C
=
2\pi r
}</md>
</p>

<p>or</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
C
=
\pi d.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circle-area-reference">
<title>Circle Area</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-arc-length-circle-reference">
<title>Arc Length</title>
<p>For central angle <m>\theta</m> measured in radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s
=
r\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>\theta</m> is in degrees,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s
=
\frac{\theta}{360^\circ}
(2\pi r).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-sector-area-reference">
<title>Sector Area</title>
<p>For <m>\theta</m> in radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{sector}}
=
\frac12r^2\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For degrees,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{sector}}
=
\frac{\theta}{360^\circ}
\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-chord-length-reference">
<title>Chord Length</title>
<p>For a circle of radius <m>r</m> and central angle <m>\theta</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c
=
2r
\sin
\left(
\frac{\theta}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>\theta</m> may be interpreted consistently in either angular unit when
used inside the corresponding trigonometric function convention.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circular-segment-area">
<title>Segment Area</title>
<p>For central angle <m>\theta</m> in radians,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{segment}}
=
\frac12r^2
(\theta-\sin\theta).
}</md>
</p>

<p>This equals sector area minus triangular area.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-annulus-formula">
<title>Annulus</title>
<p>For outer radius <m>R</m> and inner radius <m>r</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\pi
(R^2-r^2).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-ellipse-area-reference">
<title>Ellipse</title>
<p>For semimajor axis <m>a</m> and semiminor axis <m>b</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\pi ab.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The ellipse</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{x^2}{a^2}
+
\frac{y^2}{b^2}
=
1
}</md>
</p>

<p>has semiaxes <m>a</m> and <m>b</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-ellipse-eccentricity-reference">
<title>Ellipse Eccentricity</title>
<p>For <m>a\geq b&gt;0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c
=
\sqrt{a^2-b^2}
}</md>
</p>

<p>is the focal distance from center.</p>

<p>Eccentricity is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e
=
\frac{c}{a}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0\leq e&lt;1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parabola-standard-reference">
<title>Parabola Standard Form</title>
<p>A horizontal-axis parabola may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(y-k)^2
=
4p(x-h).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its vertex is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(h,k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its focus is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(h+p,k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its directrix is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x=h-p.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-vertical-parabola-standard">
<title>Vertical Parabola Standard Form</title>
<p>A vertical parabola may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(x-h)^2
=
4p(y-k).
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its focus is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(h,k+p),
}</md>
</p>

<p>and directrix is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=k-p.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-hyperbola-standard-reference">
<title>Hyperbola Standard Form</title>
<p>A horizontal hyperbola has form</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{(x-h)^2}{a^2}
-
\frac{(y-k)^2}{b^2}
=
1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its asymptotes are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y-k
=
\pm
\frac{b}{a}
(x-h).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-hyperbola-focal-relation">
<title>Hyperbola Focal Relation</title>
<p>For a hyperbola,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c^2
=
a^2+b^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Its eccentricity is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
e
=
\frac{c}{a}
&gt;
1.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-coordinate-distance-formula">
<title>Coordinate Distance Formula</title>
<p>For points</p>

<p>
<md>P_1=(x_1,y_1),
\qquad
P_2=(x_2,y_2),</md>
</p>

<p>distance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{
(x_2-x_1)^2
+
(y_2-y_1)^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-three-dimensional-distance">
<title>Three-Dimensional Distance</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>P_1=(x_1,y_1,z_1)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>P_2=(x_2,y_2,z_2),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{
(x_2-x_1)^2
+
(y_2-y_1)^2
+
(z_2-z_1)^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-midpoint-formula-reference">
<title>Midpoint Formula</title>
<p>For endpoints</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1),
\qquad
(x_2,y_2),</md>
</p>

<p>the midpoint is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M
=
\left(
\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},
\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>In three dimensions,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M
=
\left(
\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},
\frac{y_1+y_2}{2},
\frac{z_1+z_2}{2}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-slope-reference">
<title>Slope</title>
<p>For distinct points with <m>x_2\neq x_1</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m
=
\frac{
y_2-y_1
}{
x_2-x_1
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Slope measures vertical change divided by horizontal change.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-slope-intercept-reference">
<title>Slope-Intercept Form</title>
<p>A nonvertical line may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=mx+b.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here</p>

<p>
<md>m
=
\text{slope}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>b
=
\text{\(y\)-intercept}.</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-point-slope-reference">
<title>Point-Slope Form</title>
<p>A line with slope <m>m</m> passing through <m>(x_1,y_1)</m> satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y-y_1
=
m(x-x_1).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-two-point-line-reference">
<title>Two-Point Form</title>
<p>For <m>x_1\neq x_2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y-y_1
=
\frac{
y_2-y_1
}{
x_2-x_1
}
(x-x_1).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-standard-line-reference">
<title>Standard Line Form</title>
<p>A line may be written</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Ax+By=C.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>B\neq0</m>, then slope is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m
=
-\frac{A}{B}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parallel-lines-reference">
<title>Parallel Lines</title>
<p>Two distinct nonvertical lines are parallel when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m_1=m_2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In vector form, their direction vectors are scalar multiples.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-perpendicular-lines-reference">
<title>Perpendicular Lines</title>
<p>For nonvertical, nonhorizontal lines,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m_1m_2=-1.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus their slopes are negative reciprocals.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-point-line-distance">
<title>Distance From a Point to a Line</title>
<p>For point <m>(x_0,y_0)</m> and line</p>

<p>
<md>Ax+By+C=0,</md>
</p>

<p>distance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\frac{
|Ax_0+By_0+C|
}{
\sqrt{A^2+B^2}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circle-equation-reference">
<title>Equation of a Circle</title>
<p>A circle with center</p>

<p>
<md>(h,k)</md>
</p>

<p>and radius <m>r</m> satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(x-h)^2
+
(y-k)^2
=
r^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>At the origin,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x^2+y^2=r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-sphere-equation-reference">
<title>Equation of a Sphere</title>
<p>A sphere centered at</p>

<p>
<md>(h,k,\ell)</md>
</p>

<p>with radius <m>r</m> satisfies</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(x-h)^2
+
(y-k)^2
+
(z-\ell)^2
=
r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-coordinate-triangle-area">
<title>Area of a Triangle From Coordinates</title>
<p>For vertices</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1),
\quad
(x_2,y_2),
\quad
(x_3,y_3),</md>
</p>

<p>the area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
\left|
x_1(y_2-y_3)
+
x_2(y_3-y_1)
+
x_3(y_1-y_2)
\right|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-area-determinant">
<title>Determinant Form of Triangle Area</title>
<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
\left|
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
x_1 &amp; y_1 &amp; 1\\
x_2 &amp; y_2 &amp; 1\\
x_3 &amp; y_3 &amp; 1
\end{bmatrix}
\right|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-shoelace-formula-reference">
<title>Shoelace Formula</title>
<p>For a simple polygon with ordered vertices</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1),
\ldots,
(x_n,y_n),</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(x_{n+1},y_{n+1})
=
(x_1,y_1),</md>
</p>

<p>the area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
\left|
\sum_{i=1}^{n}
(x_iy_{i+1}-y_ix_{i+1})
\right|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-vector-length-geometric-reference">
<title>Vector Length</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{v}
=
(v_1,\ldots,v_n),</md>
</p>

<p>the Euclidean length is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\|\mathbf{v}\|
=
\sqrt{
v_1^2+\cdots+v_n^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-angle-between-vectors-reference">
<title>Angle Between Vectors</title>
<p>For nonzero vectors <m>\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\cos\theta
=
\frac{
\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
}{
\|\mathbf{u}\|
\|\mathbf{v}\|
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Therefore,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\theta
=
\arccos
\left(
\frac{
\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
}{
\|\mathbf{u}\|
\|\mathbf{v}\|
}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-orthogonal-vectors-reference">
<title>Orthogonal Vectors</title>
<p>Two vectors are perpendicular when</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}=0.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-vector-projection-reference">
<title>Projection of One Vector Onto Another</title>
<p>The projection of <m>\mathbf{u}</m> onto nonzero <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\operatorname{proj}_{\mathbf{v}}\mathbf{u}
=
\frac{
\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{v}
}{
\mathbf{v}\cdot\mathbf{v}
}
\mathbf{v}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parallelogram-vector-area">
<title>Area of a Parallelogram Using Vectors</title>
<p>For vectors <m>\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}\in\mathbb{R}^3</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\|\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}\|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>\|\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}\|
=
\|\mathbf{u}\|
\|\mathbf{v}\|
\sin\theta,</md>
</p>

<p>this agrees with the familiar formula <m>ab\sin\theta</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-vector-area">
<title>Triangle Area Using Vectors</title>
<p>For vectors <m>\mathbf{u},\mathbf{v}</m> forming two sides of a triangle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
\|\mathbf{u}\times\mathbf{v}\|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-scalar-triple-product-volume">
<title>Scalar Triple Product Volume</title>
<p>The volume of the parallelepiped generated by</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{u},
\mathbf{v},
\mathbf{w}</md>
</p>

<p>is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
|
\mathbf{u}\cdot
(\mathbf{v}\times\mathbf{w})
|.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\left|
\det
\begin{bmatrix}
u_1 &amp; u_2 &amp; u_3\\
v_1 &amp; v_2 &amp; v_3\\
w_1 &amp; w_2 &amp; w_3
\end{bmatrix}
\right|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-plane-equation-reference">
<title>Equation of a Plane</title>
<p>A plane with normal vector</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{n}
=
(A,B,C)</md>
</p>

<p>through point</p>

<p>
<md>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</md>
</p>

<p>has equation</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A(x-x_0)
+
B(y-y_0)
+
C(z-z_0)
=
0.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalent standard form is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
Ax+By+Cz=D.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-point-plane-distance">
<title>Distance From a Point to a Plane</title>
<p>For point</p>

<p>
<md>(x_0,y_0,z_0)</md>
</p>

<p>and plane</p>

<p>
<md>Ax+By+Cz+D=0,</md>
</p>

<p>distance is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\frac{
|Ax_0+By_0+Cz_0+D|
}{
\sqrt{A^2+B^2+C^2}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-rectangular-prism-formulas">
<title>Rectangular Prism</title>
<p>For side lengths <m>l,w,h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V=lwh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
2lw+2lh+2wh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Space diagonal is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{
l^2+w^2+h^2
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-cube-formulas">
<title>Cube</title>
<p>For side <m>s</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V=s^3,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S=6s^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and space diagonal</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d=s\sqrt3.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-general-prism-formulas">
<title>General Prism</title>
<p>For base area <m>B</m> and perpendicular height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V=Bh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a right prism with base perimeter <m>P</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
Ph.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Total surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{total}}
=
Ph+2B.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-cylinder-formulas">
<title>Cylinder</title>
<p>For radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\pi r^2h.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Lateral surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
2\pi rh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Total surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{total}}
=
2\pi rh+2\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-pyramid-formulas">
<title>General Pyramid</title>
<p>For base area <m>B</m> and perpendicular height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac13Bh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a regular pyramid with base perimeter <m>P</m> and slant height <m>\ell</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
\frac12P\ell.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-cone-formulas">
<title>Cone</title>
<p>For radius <m>r</m> and height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac13\pi r^2h.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a right circular cone, slant height is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ell
=
\sqrt{r^2+h^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Lateral surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
\pi r\ell.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Total surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{total}}
=
\pi r\ell+\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-sphere-formulas">
<title>Sphere</title>
<p>For radius <m>r</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac43\pi r^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
4\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-hemisphere-formulas">
<title>Hemisphere</title>
<p>Volume is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac23\pi r^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Curved surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{curved}}
=
2\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Including the circular base,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{total}}
=
3\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-cone-frustum-formulas">
<title>Frustum of a Cone</title>
<p>For lower radius <m>R</m>, upper radius <m>r</m>, and height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac{\pi h}{3}
\left(
R^2+Rr+r^2
\right).
}</md>
</p>

<p>The slant height is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\ell
=
\sqrt{
h^2+(R-r)^2
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Lateral area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
\pi(R+r)\ell.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-pyramid-frustum-volume">
<title>Frustum of a Pyramid</title>
<p>For base areas <m>B_1,B_2</m> and height <m>h</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac{h}{3}
\left(
B_1
+
\sqrt{B_1B_2}
+
B_2
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-torus-formulas">
<title>Torus</title>
<p>For major radius <m>R</m> and minor radius <m>r</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
2\pi^2Rr^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Surface area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
4\pi^2Rr.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>R</m> is the distance from the center of the tube to the center of the
torus.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-regular-tetrahedron-formulas">
<title>Regular Tetrahedron</title>
<p>For side length <m>a</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
\sqrt3\,a^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Volume is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac{a^3}{6\sqrt2}
=
\frac{\sqrt2}{12}a^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Height is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
h
=
a\sqrt{\frac23}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-similarity-scaling-reference">
<title>Similarity</title>
<p>If two figures are similar with length scale factor</p>

<p>
<md>k,</md>
</p>

<p>then corresponding lengths scale by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
k.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Areas scale by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
k^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Volumes scale by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
k^3.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-worked-geometric-scaling">
<title>Worked Example: Scaling</title>
<example>
<title>Scaling a Sphere</title>
<p>Suppose the radius of a sphere is doubled.</p>

<p>The radius scale factor is</p>

<p>
<md>k=2.</md>
</p>

<p>Surface area scales by</p>

<p>
<md>k^2=4.</md>
</p>

<p>Volume scales by</p>

<p>
<md>k^3=8.</md>
</p>
<p><em>Solution.</em></p>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{surface area becomes }4\text{ times as large},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{volume becomes }8\text{ times as large}.
}</md>
</p>
</example>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-law-of-sines-reference">
<title>Law of Sines</title>
<p>For triangle sides <m>a,b,c</m> opposite angles <m>A,B,C</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{a}{\sin A}
=
\frac{b}{\sin B}
=
\frac{c}{\sin C}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>If <m>R</m> is circumradius,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\frac{a}{\sin A}
=
\frac{b}{\sin B}
=
\frac{c}{\sin C}
=
2R.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-law-of-cosines-reference">
<title>Law of Cosines</title>
<p>For a triangle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c^2
=
a^2+b^2-2ab\cos C.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a^2
=
b^2+c^2-2bc\cos A
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
b^2
=
a^2+c^2-2ac\cos B.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The Pythagorean theorem is the special case <m>C=90^\circ</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-circumradius">
<title>Triangle Circumradius</title>
<p>For side lengths <m>a,b,c</m> and area <m>A</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R
=
\frac{abc}{4A}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>R</m> is the circumradius.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-inradius">
<title>Triangle Inradius</title>
<p>If <m>A</m> is triangle area and</p>

<p>
<md>s
=
\frac{a+b+c}{2}</md>
</p>

<p>is semiperimeter, then inradius is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
r
=
\frac{A}{s}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Equivalently,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=rs.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-triangle-centroid-reference">
<title>Centroid of a Triangle</title>
<p>For vertices</p>

<p>
<md>(x_1,y_1),
\quad
(x_2,y_2),
\quad
(x_3,y_3),</md>
</p>

<p>the centroid is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
G
=
\left(
\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}{3},
\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}{3}
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-tetrahedron-centroid-reference">
<title>Centroid of a Tetrahedron</title>
<p>For vertices</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{v}_1,
\mathbf{v}_2,
\mathbf{v}_3,
\mathbf{v}_4,</md>
</p>

<p>the centroid is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbf{G}
=
\frac14
\left(
\mathbf{v}_1+
\mathbf{v}_2+
\mathbf{v}_3+
\mathbf{v}_4
\right).
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-polar-coordinate-formulas">
<title>Polar Coordinates</title>
<p>The coordinate transformations are</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x=r\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=r\sin\theta.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Conversely,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
r
=
\sqrt{x^2+y^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For <m>x\neq0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\tan\theta
=
\frac{y}{x},</md>
</p>

<p>with quadrant information used to determine the correct angle.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-cylindrical-coordinate-formulas">
<title>Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
<p>Cylindrical coordinates satisfy</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x=r\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y=r\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
z=z.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
r^2=x^2+y^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-spherical-coordinate-formulas">
<title>Spherical Coordinates</title>
<p>Using the convention where <m>\rho</m> is radial distance,
<m>\theta</m> is azimuth, and <m>\phi</m> is polar angle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x
=
\rho\sin\phi\cos\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y
=
\rho\sin\phi\sin\theta,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
z
=
\rho\cos\phi.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Also,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\rho
=
\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Spherical-coordinate conventions vary, so the angle definitions should
always be stated.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parametric-line-reference">
<title>Parametric Line</title>
<p>A line through point <m>\mathbf{r}_0</m> with direction <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\mathbf{r}(t)
=
\mathbf{r}_0+t\mathbf{v}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>In coordinates,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x=x_0+at,
\qquad
y=y_0+bt,
\qquad
z=z_0+ct.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-circle-parametrization-reference">
<title>Circle Parametrization</title>
<p>A circle of radius <m>r</m> centered at <m>(h,k)</m> can be parameterized by</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x(t)
=
h+r\cos t,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y(t)
=
k+r\sin t.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For one full traversal,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
0\leq t\leq2\pi.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-ellipse-parametrization-reference">
<title>Ellipse Parametrization</title>
<p>An ellipse centered at <m>(h,k)</m> with semiaxes <m>a,b</m> has parametrization</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
x(t)
=
h+a\cos t,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y(t)
=
k+b\sin t.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parametric-arc-length-reference">
<title>Arc Length of a Plane Parametric Curve</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>x=x(t),
\qquad
y=y(t),</md>
</p>

<p>with <m>a\leq t\leq b</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L
=
\int_a^b
\sqrt{
\left(
\frac{dx}{dt}
\right)^2
+
\left(
\frac{dy}{dt}
\right)^2
}
\,dt.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-spatial-arc-length-reference">
<title>Arc Length of a Spatial Curve</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{r}(t)
=
(x(t),y(t),z(t)),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L
=
\int_a^b
\|\mathbf{r}'(t)\|
\,dt.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Thus</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L
=
\int_a^b
\sqrt{
(x')^2+(y')^2+(z')^2
}
\,dt.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-surface-area-revolution-x">
<title>Surface Area of Revolution About the <m>x</m>-Axis</title>
<p>For <m>y=f(x)\geq0</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
2\pi
\int_a^b
f(x)
\sqrt{
1+[f'(x)]^2
}
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-surface-area-revolution-y">
<title>Surface Area of Revolution About the <m>y</m>-Axis</title>
<p>For <m>x\geq0</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S
=
2\pi
\int_a^b
x
\sqrt{
1+[f'(x)]^2
}
\,dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>y=f(x)</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-disk-washer-volume-reference">
<title>Volume by Disks and Washers</title>
<p>For rotation about the <m>x</m>-axis,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\pi
\int_a^b
\left[
R(x)^2-r(x)^2
\right]
dx.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Here <m>R</m> is outer radius and <m>r</m> is inner radius.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-shell-volume-reference">
<title>Volume by Cylindrical Shells</title>
<p>For shells of radius <m>r(x)</m> and height <m>h(x)</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
2\pi
\int_a^b
r(x)h(x)\,dx.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-polar-area-reference">
<title>Area in Polar Coordinates</title>
<p>For polar curve</p>

<p>
<md>r=f(\theta),</md>
</p>

<p>area between <m>\theta=\alpha</m> and <m>\theta=\beta</m> is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12
\int_\alpha^\beta
r^2\,d\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-polar-arc-length-reference">
<title>Polar Arc Length</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>r=r(\theta),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L
=
\int_\alpha^\beta
\sqrt{
r^2
+
\left(
\frac{dr}{d\theta}
\right)^2
}
\,d\theta.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-plane-curvature-reference">
<title>Curvature of a Plane Curve</title>
<p>For <m>y=f(x)</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa
=
\frac{
|f''(x)|
}{
\left(
1+[f'(x)]^2
\right)^{3/2}
}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The radius of curvature is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
R
=
\frac1\kappa
}</md>
</p>

<p>when <m>\kappa\neq0</m>.</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-parametric-curvature-reference">
<title>Curvature of a Parametric Plane Curve</title>
<p>For</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{r}(t)
=
(x(t),y(t)),</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa
=
\frac{
|x'y''-y'x''|
}{
\left[
(x')^2+(y')^2
\right]^{3/2}
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-space-curve-curvature-reference">
<title>Curvature of a Space Curve</title>
<p>For a sufficiently regular curve,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\kappa
=
\frac{
\|\mathbf{r}'\times\mathbf{r}''\|
}{
\|\mathbf{r}'\|^3
}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-quick-plane-geometry-table">
<title>Quick Plane Geometry Table</title>
<p>\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
<em>Shape</em>
&amp;
<em>Area</em>
&amp;
<em>Perimeter / Circumference</em>
\\ \hline</p>

<p>Square
&amp;
<m>s^2</m>
&amp;
<m>4s</m>
\\</p>

<p>Rectangle
&amp;
<m>lw</m>
&amp;
<m>2(l+w)</m>
\\</p>

<p>Triangle
&amp;
<m>\frac12bh</m>
&amp;
<m>a+b+c</m>
\\</p>

<p>Parallelogram
&amp;
<m>bh</m>
&amp;
<m>2(a+b)</m>
\\</p>

<p>Trapezoid
&amp;
<m>\frac12(b_1+b_2)h</m>
&amp;
sum of sides
\\</p>

<p>Rhombus
&amp;
<m>\frac12d_1d_2</m>
&amp;
<m>4s</m>
\\</p>

<p>Circle
&amp;
<m>\pi r^2</m>
&amp;
<m>2\pi r</m>
\end{tabular}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-quick-solid-geometry-table">
<title>Quick Solid Geometry Table</title>
<p>\begin{tabular}{c|c|c}
<em>Solid</em>
&amp;
<em>Volume</em>
&amp;
<em>Surface Area</em>
\\ \hline</p>

<p>Cube
&amp;
<m>s^3</m>
&amp;
<m>6s^2</m>
\\</p>

<p>Rectangular prism
&amp;
<m>lwh</m>
&amp;
<m>2lw+2lh+2wh</m>
\\</p>

<p>Cylinder
&amp;
<m>\pi r^2h</m>
&amp;
<m>2\pi rh+2\pi r^2</m>
\\</p>

<p>Cone
&amp;
<m>\frac13\pi r^2h</m>
&amp;
<m>\pi r\ell+\pi r^2</m>
\\</p>

<p>Sphere
&amp;
<m>\frac43\pi r^3</m>
&amp;
<m>4\pi r^2</m>
\\</p>

<p>Pyramid
&amp;
<m>\frac13Bh</m>
&amp;
depends on base and slant height
\end{tabular}</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-quick-coordinate-geometry-table">
<title>Quick Coordinate Geometry Table</title>
<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{
(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
M
=
\left(
\frac{x_1+x_2}{2},
\frac{y_1+y_2}{2}
\right)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m
=
\frac{
y_2-y_1
}{
x_2-x_1
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
y-y_1
=
m(x-x_1)
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
(x-h)^2+(y-k)^2=r^2
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-geometric-formulas-common-errors">
<title>Common Errors</title>
<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-diameter-where-radius-is-required">
<title>Using Diameter Where Radius Is Required</title>
<p>Circle area is</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=\pi r^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>not</p>

<p>
<md>\pi d^2.</md>
</p>

<p>Since</p>

<p>
<md>r=\frac d2,</md>
</p>

<p>one may write</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac{\pi d^2}{4}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-to-square-the-radius">
<title>Forgetting to Square the Radius</title>
<p>Circumference is linear in radius:</p>

<p>
<md>C=2\pi r.</md>
</p>

<p>Area is quadratic:</p>

<p>
<md>A=\pi r^2.</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-area-and-perimeter">
<title>Confusing Area and Perimeter</title>
<p>Perimeter has units of length.</p>

<p>Area has units of length squared.</p>

<p>For example,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
P
\text{ has units }L,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
\text{ has units }L^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-confusing-surface-area-and-volume">
<title>Confusing Surface Area and Volume</title>
<p>Surface area scales as</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>while volume scales as</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
L^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Their units should never be interchanged.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-slant-height-as-perpendicular-height">
<title>Using Slant Height as Perpendicular Height</title>
<p>Cone volume uses perpendicular height:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
=
\frac13\pi r^2h.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Surface area uses slant height:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S_{\mathrm{lateral}}
=
\pi r\ell.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-degrees-in-s-r">
<title>Using Degrees in <m>s=r\theta</m></title>
<p>The formula</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s=r\theta
}</md>
</p>

<p>requires <m>\theta</m> in radians.</p>

<p>If degrees are used, convert first or use the degree-based fraction of a full
circle.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-degrees-in-12r-2">
<title>Using Degrees in <m>\frac12r^2\theta</m></title>
<p>Similarly,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A_{\mathrm{sector}}
=
\frac12r^2\theta
}</md>
</p>

<p>requires radians.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-forgetting-absolute-value-in-coordinate-area">
<title>Forgetting Absolute Value in Coordinate Area</title>
<p>The determinant or shoelace expression may be negative depending on vertex
orientation.</p>

<p>Geometric area is nonnegative:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12|\text{signed determinant}|.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-the-pythagorean-theorem-on-a-nonright-triangle">
<title>Using the Pythagorean Theorem on a Nonright Triangle</title>
<p>The formula</p>

<p>
<md>a^2+b^2=c^2</md>
</p>

<p>requires a right angle.</p>

<p>For a general triangle use the Law of Cosines.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-mixing-semiperimeter-and-perimeter-in-heron-s-formula">
<title>Mixing Semiperimeter and Perimeter in Heron's Formula</title>
<p>Heron's formula uses</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
s
=
\frac{a+b+c}{2},
}</md>
</p>

<p>not the full perimeter.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-using-the-wrong-hyperbola-relation">
<title>Using the Wrong Hyperbola Relation</title>
<p>For an ellipse,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c^2=a^2-b^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a hyperbola,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
c^2=a^2+b^2.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-assuming-all-spherical-coordinate-conventions-match">
<title>Assuming All Spherical Coordinate Conventions Match</title>
<p>Some texts interchange the meanings of <m>\theta</m> and <m>\phi</m>.</p>

<p>Always check the stated convention.</p>
</subsubsection>

<subsubsection xml:id="subsubsec-ignoring-scale-dimensions">
<title>Ignoring Scale Dimensions</title>
<p>Doubling a length does not merely double area or volume.</p>

<p>If <m>k=2</m>,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
\mapsto
4A,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V
\mapsto
8V.
}</md>
</p>
</subsubsection>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-geometric-formulas-exercises">
<title>Exercises</title>
<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Convert <m>150^\circ</m> to radians.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Convert <m>7\pi/6</m> radians to degrees.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the hypotenuse of a right triangle with legs <m>8</m> and <m>15</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area of a triangle with base <m>12</m> and height <m>7</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the perimeter and area of a rectangle with dimensions <m>5</m> by <m>9</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the diagonal of a square with side <m>10</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the circumference and area of a circle with radius <m>6</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the arc length subtended by angle <m>\pi/3</m> in a circle of radius <m>9</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area of a sector with radius <m>4</m> and central angle <m>5\pi/6</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use Heron's formula for a triangle with sides <m>5,5,6</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Law of Cosines to find the third side of a triangle with sides
<m>7</m>, <m>10</m>, and included angle <m>60^\circ</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the Law of Sines to find a missing side in a nonright triangle.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area of a regular hexagon with side length <m>4</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the distance between</p>

<p>
<md>(2,-1)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(8,7).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the midpoint between</p>

<p>
<md>(-4,3)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(6,-5).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the slope of the line through</p>

<p>
<md>(1,2)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(5,10).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the equation of a line with slope <m>3</m> through <m>(2,-1)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the distance from <m>(3,4)</m> to the line</p>

<p>
<md>2x-y+5=0.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Write the equation of a circle centered at <m>(3,-2)</m> with radius <m>5</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area of the triangle with vertices</p>

<p>
<md>(0,0),
\quad
(4,0),
\quad
(1,3).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the shoelace formula to compute the area of a quadrilateral.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume and surface area of a cube with side <m>6</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume of a cylinder with radius <m>3</m> and height <m>10</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the surface area of the same cylinder.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume of a cone with radius <m>5</m> and height <m>12</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the slant height and surface area of the same cone.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume and surface area of a sphere with radius <m>7</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>A solid is enlarged by scale factor <m>3</m>. Determine the area and volume
scale factors.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the angle between</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{u}=(1,2)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{v}=(3,-1).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Determine whether</p>

<p>
<md>(2,3,-1)</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>(1,-1,-1)</md>
</p>

<p>are orthogonal.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute the projection of</p>

<p>
<md>(3,4)</md>
</p>

<p>onto</p>

<p>
<md>(1,0).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the area of the parallelogram generated by</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{u}=(1,2,3),
\qquad
\mathbf{v}=(2,-1,1).</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the volume of the parallelepiped generated by three specified vectors.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the equation of the plane through <m>(1,2,3)</m> with normal
<m>(2,-1,4)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the distance from a point to a specified plane.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Convert the point</p>

<p>
<md>(3,3)</md>
</p>

<p>from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Convert</p>

<p>
<md>(r,\theta)
=
\left(
4,\frac{\pi}{6}
\right)</md>
</p>

<p>to rectangular coordinates.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Parameterize a circle of radius <m>5</m> centered at <m>(2,1)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Parameterize an ellipse with semiaxes <m>4</m> and <m>2</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Compute arc length of</p>

<p>
<md>\mathbf{r}(t)
=
(t,t^2),
\qquad
0\leq t\leq1.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find polar area enclosed by</p>

<p>
<md>r=2</md>
</p>

<p>over one full revolution.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Verify that the polar-area formula reproduces <m>\pi r^2</m> for a circle.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use the disk method to derive the volume of a sphere.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Use cylindrical shells to compute the volume of a specified solid of
revolution.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the curvature of</p>

<p>
<md>y=x^2</md>
</p>

<p>at <m>x=0</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Find the centroid of a triangle from its coordinates.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the circumradius relation</p>

<p>
<md>R=\frac{abc}{4A}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Show that the area of a triangle is</p>

<p>
<md>A=rs,</md>
</p>

<p>where <m>r</m> is inradius and <m>s</m> is semiperimeter.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive Heron's formula.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the regular polygon area formula</p>

<p>
<md>A
=
\frac{ns^2}{4\tan(\pi/n)}.</md>
</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the cone volume formula using integration.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the sphere surface-area formula.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the sphere volume formula.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the conical frustum volume formula.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the shoelace formula using determinants.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Prove the scalar triple product gives parallelepiped volume.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the formula for distance from a point to a plane.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise>
<statement>
<p>Derive the curvature formula for a plane graph <m>y=f(x)</m>.</p>
</statement>
</exercise>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-geometric-formulas-summary">
<title>Conceptual Summary</title>
<p>Geometry connects algebraic measurements with spatial structure.</p>

<p>For right triangles,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
a^2+b^2=c^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For a triangle,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A
=
\frac12bh.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For circles,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
C=2\pi r
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
A=\pi r^2.
}</md>
</p>

<p>For spheres,</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
S=4\pi r^2
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
V=\frac43\pi r^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Coordinate geometry uses</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
d
=
\sqrt{
(x_2-x_1)^2+(y_2-y_1)^2
}
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
m
=
\frac{y_2-y_1}{x_2-x_1}.
}</md>
</p>

<p>Vector geometry extends these formulas through dot products, cross products,
and determinants.</p>

<p>Scaling by a length factor <m>k</m> changes</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{length by }k,
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{area by }k^2,
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{volume by }k^3.
}</md>
</p>

<p>The most important practical principles are:</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{use consistent units},
}</md>
</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{distinguish length, area, and volume},
}</md>
</p>

<p>and</p>

<p>
<md>\boxed{
\text{identify which geometric measurements each formula requires}.
}</md>
</p>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-06-geometric-formulas-subsec-geometric-formulas-connections">
<title>Section Connections</title>
<remark>
<title>Chapter Connection</title>
<p>Geometric Formulas consolidates the Euclidean, coordinate, vector, analytic,
and solid-geometry relationships used throughout the textbook. Distance,
slope, midpoint, circle, and conic formulas connect with Algebra and
Precalculus; triangle formulas connect with Trigonometry; vector area and
volume formulas connect with Linear Algebra and Vector Calculus; arc length,
surface area, and volumes of revolution connect with Calculus; curvature
connects with Differential Geometry; and coordinate transformations connect
with multivariable analysis and mathematical physics. The next reference
section, Trigonometric Identities, collects reciprocal, quotient,
Pythagorean, cofunction, sum-and-difference, double-angle, half-angle,
product-to-sum, sum-to-product, and inverse-trigonometric relationships.</p>
</remark>
</subsection>

</section>
